{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Philadelphia Phillies arrived with championship expectations and spent the first half of the season doing everything in their power to justify them. A blistering April (19-9) set the tone immediately, driven by unexpected heroes like Cristian Pache delivering clutch hits and the offense proving capable of dramatic rallies — including a wild late April game against the Angels where Johan Rojas launched his first career homer to cap a comeback. The team's depth and lineup flexibility were on full display, and Philadelphia looked every bit like a World Series contender.\n\nMay continued the momentum with a 20-7 record — the best month of the season — featuring a parade of improbable contributors. Kody Clemens delivered a stunning go-ahead homer, Edmundo Sosa laced a key triple, and even a hit-by-pitch with the bases loaded proved decisive against the Mets. The bullpen had its rough nights, particularly José Alvarado surrendering a costly blast, but the offense bailed out the pitching staff more often than not. This was a team that knew how to win close games.\n\nJune began to show some cracks — a 15-11 record that featured a deflating double play by Nick Castellanos that killed a rally, though Alec Bohm's clutch two-run double against Baltimore offered a reminder of what this lineup could do. Then came July, where the Phillies stumbled to 10-14, their only losing month. Matt Strahm surrendering a Jazz Chisholm grand slam to the Yankees in late July crystallized the skid. August offered partial redemption (15-13), anchored by Schwarber's grand slam and Castellanos coming alive, and a strong September push (15-11) saw J.T. Realmuto, Cal Stevenson, and the rest of the cast re-establish the Phillies as a legitimate October threat.\n\nThe postseason brought the Mets and a NLDS that would linger painfully into the offseason. Game 2 featured the kind of breathtaking back-and-forth the Citizens Bank Park faithful had dreamed of — Harper and Castellanos homering, Bryson Stott tripling in two runs — but the Mets refused to die, with Mark Vientos repeatedly inflicting damage off the Philadelphia bullpen. A Francisco Lindor grand slam off Carlos Estévez in the decisive moment sealed the Phillies' fate. A 95-win season, a strong roster, and genuine talent weren't enough — the bullpen, which had been the team's Achilles heel in moments all year, came up short when the stakes were highest.",
  "chapters": [
